🧭 SmartNav: An Indoor Navigation Demonstrator

SmartNav is an educational Android application designed to demonstrate and compare two fundamental indoor navigation techniques:

Dead Reckoning (DR)

Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM)

Since GPS performs poorly indoors, SmartNav showcases how these alternative methods can be used to track user movement inside buildings.

The application is built entirely in Kotlin and serves as a hands-on demonstration of sensor fusion and computer vision concepts, making it ideal for students and developers interested in robotics, navigation, and AR-based systems.

📌 Core Concepts Explained

SmartNav visualizes the difference between navigation with and without external visual references.

🚶 Dead Reckoning (DR)

Dead Reckoning estimates the current position using a known starting point and incrementally updates it based on movement data such as direction and distance traveled.

🔍 Analogy

Imagine walking in a dark room:

You know where you started.

You count your steps and turns.

Over time, small errors in step length or direction accumulate, causing your estimated position to drift.

This accumulated error is known as sensor drift.

🧠 Implementation in SmartNav

Implemented in DeadReckoningEngine.kt, the DR system uses:

Accelerometer → Detects steps via rhythmic force spikes.

Orientation Sensors → Determines heading using sensor fusion.

Fixed Step Length → Each detected step moves the user forward by a constant distance (e.g., 0.75 m).

This approach is a classic Pedestrian Dead Reckoning (PDR) method.

🗺️ Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM)

SLAM allows a device to build a map of an unknown environment while tracking its own position within that map at the same time.

🔍 Analogy

Imagine exploring a cave while sketching landmarks:

You draw distinctive features as you see them.

When you revisit a landmark, you refine both your map and your position.

Localization and mapping happen simultaneously.

🧠 Implementation in SmartNav

Implemented in OrbSlamTracker.kt, the SLAM system:

Uses the camera to detect visual features (ORB keypoints).

Matches features between consecutive frames to estimate motion.

Stores features as MapPoint objects to build a sparse 3D map.

Relies heavily on ARCore, which provides high-accuracy visual-inertial odometry.

ARCore acts as the primary source of truth, ensuring a stable and accurate SLAM trajectory.

⚙️ How SmartNav Works

Both Dead Reckoning and SLAM trackers run simultaneously.

Their estimated paths are plotted on the screen in real time.

Over time:

The SLAM trajectory remains accurate.

The Dead Reckoning path drifts, clearly demonstrating cumulative sensor error.

This side-by-side visualization provides a powerful demonstration of why SLAM is essential for long-term indoor navigation.

🛠️ Technologies Used

Kotlin – 100% Kotlin codebase.

Android Jetpack – ViewModel, LiveData, Fragments.

ARCore – Visual-inertial SLAM and environment understanding.

SceneView (ARSceneView) – Simplifies ARCore session handling and rendering.

🌍 Real-World Use Cases

Although SmartNav is a demonstration app, the underlying technology applies to:

🏢 Indoor Navigation – Airports, malls, campuses.

🏭 Industrial Logistics – Factory floors, warehouses.

🚒 Emergency Response – Navigation in low-visibility environments.

🎮 Augmented Reality – Persistent placement of virtual objects.

📱 Device Compatibility

⚠️ ARCore support is mandatory

This application requires an ARCore-compatible Android device.
ARCore is essential for SLAM functionality.

📄 Supported devices list:
https://developers.google.com/ar/devices
